<script>
  import iee754 from '../assets/ieee-754.svg?raw';
</script>

The implementation looks a little peculiar due to the nature of 64-bit floats `f64`.
Though `f64` serves to model the real numbers $\bbR$, they are discretely encoded by 64 bits like so.
$$
  \begin{aligned}
    f(b_0, \ldots, b_{63}) &= (-1)^{b_{63}} \Big( 1 + d(b_0, \ldots, b_{51}) \Big) 2^{E(b_{52}, \ldots, b_{62}) - 1023} \\
    d(b_0, \ldots, b_{51}) &= \sum_{i=1}^{52} b_{52-i} 2^{-i} \\
    E(b_{52}, \ldots, b_{62}) &= \sum_{i=0}^{10} b_{52+i} 2^{i}
  \end{aligned}
$$
We see that the float $f(b_0, \ldots, b_{63})$ is decomposed into by a _decimal_ or _fractional_ number,
$$
  d(b_0, \ldots, b_{51}) \in [1, 2),
$$
and a diadic _exponent_ $E(b_{52}, \ldots, b_{62})$ which provides us a binary order of magnitude.
This design is to equally allocate 52 bits of information between each diadic interval of the form $[2^n, 2^{n+1})$ or $(-2^{n+1}, -2^n]$ for positive integers $n$ representable with 11 bits of information.

<div>{@html iee754}</div>

Depending on which interval $[a, b]$ decides our `Uniform` measure, there may be more or less than $2^{52}$ instances `x: f64` satisfying `a <= x && x <= b`.
This is something we cannot control, simply due to the information available within `f64`.
For this reason, our implementation decides to sample 64 bits of information $(b_0, \ldots, b_{63})$ from `rng.next_u64`, only use 52 bits of the `u64` to produce a fractional component $d(b_0, \ldots, b_{51}) \in [1, 2)$, and apply an affine transform mapping $[1, 2)$ to $[a, b]$.
Lastly, if we scale via
```rs
let rand_in_ab = a + (rand_in_12 - 1) * (b - a)
```
the rounding rules of `f64` multiplication may produce numbers `rand_in_ab > b`.
This is why we change `scale` in the constructor `Uniform::new`.
